#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105646 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c8ebd35-d0ad-5c62-a89d-7844a7a21587" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Sine Polar Rose in the First Quadrant"}] :question/id #uuid "af255938-10ff-5169-9c5d-eac817354465" :question/math-academy-id "e-635" :question/problem "Find the integral expression corresponding to the area of the first petal of the polar curve $r = \\sin(5\\theta),$ represented by the shaded region shown below.\n\n![](images/66/66062568cedc1b46822dd7e0e53779482f1923d621b47d29f38141ff770b4498.png)" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta) = \\sin(5\\theta)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sin(5\\theta) = 0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n5\\theta = 0, \\pi \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right].$  Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{0}^{\\pi/5} \\sin^2(5\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105647 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc2f214-e7a9-5bbc-9a83-241cca04ca07" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a General Polar Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "c4c49c0d-865a-55d1-8194-35dda3907269" :question/math-academy-id "e-636" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve  $r = 1+\\sqrt{2}\\cos\\left(2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right),$ represented by the shaded region shown below.\n\n![](images/95/95138031d082cfc739c2c2569af9a9ea5c63d4b6990237b8267c4b2fe2481b09.png)" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta) = 1+\\sqrt{2}\\cos\\left(2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(1+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal extends from the third quadrant to the first quadrant. Since the petal begins in the third quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta$ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} = -\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \n\\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \n2\\theta = -\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n\\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \n\\theta = -\\dfrac{5\\pi}{8}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta  \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{5\\pi}{8}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right].\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{-5\\pi/8}^{\\pi/8}  \\left( 1+\\sqrt{2}\\cos\\left(2\\theta+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\right)^2 \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105690 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5d5ccdb-070b-5ce1-b8af-dddb87675bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Cosine Polar Rose"}] :question/id #uuid "3f355cb7-222f-5992-a212-f1b86ae37ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-9541" :question/problem "Find the interval for $\\theta$ corresponding to the first petal of the polar curve $r = \\sqrt{2}\\cos(6\\theta),$ represented by the shaded region shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e401186103f1cb3b060b99037bf5af07e873c191324759627275d77d235688c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos(6\\theta)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\cos(6\\theta) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta$ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $6\\theta\\in[-\\pi,\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n6\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{12},\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{12},\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\pi/12}^{\\pi/12} \\cos^2(6\\theta) \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5d5ccdb-070b-5ce1-b8af-dddb87675bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Cosine Polar Rose"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63855fed-118f-5138-a879-f0b81c16d4f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(4+\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544301} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0cbc70-aa26-5af9-a6bb-3e3f85dc4845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544321} {:answer/id #uuid "726eeee2-98d9-5930-919c-11fd667234f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544341} {:answer/id #uuid "83b74220-3ac7-54d2-b4de-ea3f0fdab608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544374} {:answer/id #uuid "b13fd3a4-0f4b-5c2c-b0f7-8169cad6dd54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83b74220-3ac7-54d2-b4de-ea3f0fdab608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544374} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6c931c3-cfba-5275-ba59-84cd3742ca6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f3edede-f2b8-5413-b0b9-835c57755de4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124414" :question/problem "![](images/51/51ab11405aeb635dafaeadef1b174db163f395a62126225f99de07884b3d8cb9.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $4\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n4\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5d5ccdb-070b-5ce1-b8af-dddb87675bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Cosine Polar Rose"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04aaf6f0-1ada-5467-bb53-d81bc7503a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544097} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6a6ef5-246a-53a2-81a7-26459da54ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(3+\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544256} {:answer/id #uuid "55c8f95e-9be1-5a02-b4e5-0870aeadb069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544275} {:answer/id #uuid "66fe0a64-caf0-5d4e-a637-a3c583daff6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544310} {:answer/id #uuid "6a25df31-0140-584d-8f5b-d560aebe68ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04aaf6f0-1ada-5467-bb53-d81bc7503a1e" :