or $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c8ebd35-d0ad-5c62-a89d-7844a7a21587" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Sine Polar Rose in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ec012b5-f731-5c5e-96d4-923c5b550d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544362} {:answer/id #uuid "7f594d21-de94-5f4b-a0d8-17674ad8f887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544365} {:answer/id #uuid "c0162a02-455a-5f09-a239-5b7fe41f7d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544497} {:answer/id #uuid "ccaf2d7c-3557-543d-af50-fae40376027f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544520} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc1fc67-a5a0-5126-89ee-1dd84d13b93e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}{sin}^{2}⁡(5+\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ec012b5-f731-5c5e-96d4-923c5b550d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544362} :answer-field/id #uuid "79734443-de36-5288-80ea-687e1187b2c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9a8bb7e-ad2c-5185-87eb-ce30902a04e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-124424" :question/problem "![](images/97/97b30cfa241a6e1b9db4d5332749ae503a926711c0e7c78ab188c2327463c89b.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{5}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc2f214-e7a9-5bbc-9a83-241cca04ca07" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a General Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c54c735-3838-59f7-9d18-f9c4833f0ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544222} {:answer/id #uuid "4fab53b6-6248-5945-8620-04b4fd64fbe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{3\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544260} {:answer/id #uuid "c50a1248-20d5-5e72-88fe-eb63c706b7e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544506} {:answer/id #uuid "cb989c47-35ee-5257-b346-93de6ab5fb9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544519} {:answer/id #uuid "d65fab8c-cab0-5add-bbca-2b554091e44d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{-\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c50a1248-20d5-5e72-88fe-eb63c706b7e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544506} :answer-field/id #uuid "94f7246e-f57b-594c-be96-bb72d5c5cb6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2be045cc-480f-57a5-b39f-c617f83c588d" :question/math-academy-id "q-125349" :question/problem "![](images/94/9405ee6a4b24ef36c2a59a9f4a984728716e9c281456d1459d591393d3c73720.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve\n$r=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )},$\n  represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal lies in the fourth quadrant. Since the petal lies in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta $ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $4\\theta +\\pi $ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n4\\theta +\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,4\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{8},-\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{3\\pi }{8},-\\frac{\\pi }{8}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this interval lies in the fourth quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\pi )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc2f214-e7a9-5bbc-9a83-241cca04ca07" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a General Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e39f4a-397e-573f-8267-d6dff3c45d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544201} {:answer/id #uuid "6028d4af-94c2-52de-a2e3-29fdb44dd06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544295} {:answer/id #uuid "69c80d92-4c28-5a07-8592-a922020c016a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /12}^{5\\pi /12}(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544315} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7607b6-fff2-5114-adf4-8abe60405481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544400} {:answer/id #uuid "e41bf703-af8a-5e9d-9f4f-8722fbf336bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /12}^{5\\pi /12}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e41bf703-af8a-5e9d-9f4f-8722fbf336bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /12}^{5\\pi /12}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544578} :answer-field/id #uuid "18e19576-c2ce-57ce-a908-4860ee2c8b3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3922fac2-663d-5efa-bc44-c819bbafb745" :question/math-academy-id "q-125357" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9985c6afd3e5440dbe1e51e4046e7744c9a2e01472354054796247d40ed7ed6.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve \n$r=\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),$\nrepresented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal extends from the fourth quadrant to the first quadrant. Since the petal begins in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta $ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $2\\theta $ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n2\\theta =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{5\\pi }{12},\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{5\\pi }{12},\\frac{5\\pi }{12}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /12}^{5\\pi /12}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c8ebd35-d0ad-5c62-a89d-7844a7a21587" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Sine Polar Rose in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d3ae86-fe5b-5459-9248-c229a4cb9d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544126} {:answer/id #uuid "1844ef73-602c-5b30-88ea-ffffbf088136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544139} {:answer/id #uuid "682eb163-f2ab-5256-9c37-ff22d6e12633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544313} {:answer/id #uuid "82bafe14-c2b9-5557-b37c-58363f34c732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\