s us two solutions for $7\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n7\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{7}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544849 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c8ebd35-d0ad-5c62-a89d-7844a7a21587" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Sine Polar Rose in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be217e1-8af5-5001-b379-27a9f68b7e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544112} {:answer/id #uuid "48a4b65d-2629-5920-82b0-6f811ae028d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544245} {:answer/id #uuid "988ee9dd-88bf-5f48-a619-635ec25c4e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544418} {:answer/id #uuid "b937f4f6-23c9-5053-860a-c16580c2fbb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544479} {:answer/id #uuid "e771cf3c-1b99-5030-acbb-373402951169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48a4b65d-2629-5920-82b0-6f811ae028d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544245} :answer-field/id #uuid "86333074-728b-5345-a40e-5dbc3ddeb7aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96dc50ad-7c40-5c2b-a752-a6f1cca71c8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-127794" :question/problem "![](images/b7/b7475f0a169e62121711d52daaf51e8954053fc35738266d94240b6e70de6258.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $2\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n2\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c8ebd35-d0ad-5c62-a89d-7844a7a21587" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Sine Polar Rose in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ca5b55e-64e9-57fc-988e-cb6395776e42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /9}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544146} {:answer/id #uuid "1ecded90-01b7-5d43-b3ff-b3f9fe7aed89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544154} {:answer/id #uuid "706f5975-5ca9-5baa-a6cc-d37212c61ecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544337} {:answer/id #uuid "7b31c624-e893-58d9-9540-be3f1b71efc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /9}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544359} {:answer/id #uuid "d8708299-6b74-5928-9a08-9d2da799883a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /9}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ca5b55e-64e9-57fc-988e-cb6395776e42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /9}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544146} :answer-field/id #uuid "2af88dd3-9486-5aea-a0ba-f768f6515698" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77bce5e4-123e-5ff9-af9f-15d1cb34dfd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-127796" :question/problem "![](images/19/192007bbd5aee67607897bc1e4e3e29d106c8f6041fe8b06025f48999e68fcfb.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $9\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n9\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{9}].$  Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /9}{sin}^{2}⁡(9\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5d5ccdb-070b-5ce1-b8af-dddb87675bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Cosine Polar Rose"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27ef4703-6639-5623-8c0b-2ffb2cd82831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544178} {:answer/id #uuid "42047be1-5cd9-5f0f-9112-70b8e78c7e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544233} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6a7060-6e4d-5e7b-9e87-0feb10e6a475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544325} {:answer/id #uuid "a98b5b54-648a-5f5c-abff-c03076a297d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544451} {:answer/id #uuid "e724f53d-777b-5184-bede-b852c936bd20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e724f53d-777b-5184-bede-b852c936bd20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544583} :answer-field/id #uuid "5db885ae-a359-50aa-b94a-866c4319d576" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3aec8613-8bc5-55d8-bb63-25dfe3e5bcce" :question/math-academy-id "q-127800" :question/problem "![](images/44/446278a820ba21da7f5a1f15639e8cc4f96e38f38f3b5905b926bb9d2a106948.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $6\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n6\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{12}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{cos}^{2}⁡(6\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc2f214-e7a9-5bbc-9a83-241cca04ca07" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a General Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6460b2fb-7268-53e2-8310-f6e3e9f4da9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /24}^{5\\pi /24}(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544304} {:answer/id #uuid "7455fe81-1ff5-5cd4-b856-44ac7b5375d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544345} {:answer/id #uuid "92f84b7b-a5c3-5077-a2b7-a15ef5aaed82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /24}^{5\\pi /24}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544410} {:answer/id #uuid "b7726ad9-a1d0-5c91-a3b7-f047de128038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /12}^{5\\pi /12}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544476} {:answer/id #uuid "d7293488-db7d-5465-8e92-167469afa235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92f84b7b-a5c3-5077-a2b7-a15ef5aaed82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /24}^{5\\pi /24}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544410} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d0dc722-35ee-5af2-833d-cec03bbc5f7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "841c4177-3882-5def-83ad-75d73368e31f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127997" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0befc6c40eb7b53a45f1e27d0445d5abc614937c37b36d5cbf5420e1d2b51ff9.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve \n$r=\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),$\n represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ) & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal extends from the fourth quadrant to the first quadrant. Since the petal begins in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta $ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $4\\theta $ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n4\\theta =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{5\\pi }{24},\\frac{5\\pi }{24}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{5\\pi }{24},\\frac{5\\pi }{24}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-5\\pi /24}^{5\\pi /24}{(\\sqrt{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc2f214-e7a9-5bbc-9a83-241cca04ca07" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a General Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3706d8af-6d1d-5cf1-8135-8e4636d730bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-11\\pi /12}^{\\pi /24}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544207} {:answer/id #uuid "7a839304-0eaa-5bd1-991e-aef2bfde6fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-11\\pi /24}^{\\pi /24}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544357} {:answer/id #uuid "8a565251-4df8-54ec-82e4-90426624b915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-15\\pi /24}^{\\pi /24}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544384} {:answer/id #uuid "c66fd37c-733e-5b4d-a121-7cc00eec7641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-13\\pi /24}^{\\pi /24}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}))\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544508} {:answer/id #uuid "f00673f9-7b84-5686-a930-58c169a4d2f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-11\\pi /24}^{\\pi /24}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}))\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a839304-0eaa-5bd1-991e-aef2bfde6fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-11\\pi /24}^{\\pi /24}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544357} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7a05f9d-bc78-562f-97c9-53bdd878f4a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a11f42c6-9c97-5af9-adee-a7210e67b825" :question/math-academy-id "q-128009" :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc41b37639eb7715fbecd55510222f77957c05b705bc83b93c755ad2e7cf3dcd.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve\n$r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),$\n  represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal extends from the fourth quadrant to the first quadrant. Since the petal begins in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta $ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}=-\\pi ,\\pi \\,⟹\\,4\\theta =-\\frac{11\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{11\\pi }{24},\\frac{\\pi }{24}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{11\\pi }{24},\\frac{\\pi }{24}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-11\\pi /24}^{\\pi /24}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta +\\frac{5\\pi }{6}))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5d5ccdb-070b-5ce1-b8af-dddb87675bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Cosine Polar Rose"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24532688-eec4-5621-92fa-532033042c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544165} {:answer/id #uuid "28cb0b75-f16a-56d1-a298-8e7854f23b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544181} {:answer/id #uuid "3301cca7-812b-5032-929a-442a1ea385d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /5}^{\\pi /5}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544202} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0fc7b3-5743-5f2c-83ef-929c57f0d1a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544525} {:answer/id #uuid "d84ce1a1-cfbc-5d9c-a9e0-a3de51b4f70e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28cb0b75-f16a-56d1-a298-8e7854f23b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544181} :answer-field/id #uuid "b53aa41e-e9c5-50c8-ab7e-c6508a46ac1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "790dcc10-7f22-59d7-b4bc-0f6943a2af9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-14916" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f62e6a4cec5b2a877f2491a101f3929edd6664424b86daab4bbfe8096c5c9282.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c8ebd35-d0ad-5c62-a89d-7844a7a21587" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Sine Polar Rose in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40c51311-8856-5758-9682-9586a5a3a274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544232} {:answer/id #uuid "990dde5b-5c60-56f9-aa59-5fb12862070a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544420} {:answer/id #uuid "cb36aeac-bf37-5bf6-bcca-5eac16ae3089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544518} {:answer/id #uuid "ce96ff87-2f38-5ce1-a84d-662458a4201e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544526} {:answer/id #uuid "f213dbd7-8a94-5e4a-8f52-24a730f798a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f213dbd7-8a94-5e4a-8f52-24a730f798a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544612} :answer-field/id #uuid "d21499a7-d8b9-5197-a6bf-e3d9bd7a4181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db057c50-119e-5372-a829-3c2e39e59b9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-20905" :question/problem "![](images/01/01a371be4c5a555b0d3790431111468acfed1471d16c4c4c305bcfe311ce3576.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n3\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abc2f214-e7a9-5bbc-9a83-241cca04ca07" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a General Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07480ff0-ae02-581d-86f9-7d724c53aaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544103} {:answer/id #uuid "8cbbf9ef-e697-551c-a40d-ef84f5139aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544387} {:answer/id #uuid "9eef7171-be32-50a0-8e70-e1831b5e568b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544430} {:answer/id #uuid "d52a5d39-739c-5ddf-bfd4-0166361d02cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544540} {:answer/id #uuid "decc0c03-0ede-5b99-bbde-50a8ef28dfcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "decc0c03-0ede-5b99-bbde-50a8ef28dfcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544569} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7ef8298-8d9d-5983-baab-fbc6991d3cdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a2aaba8-489e-5805-b9f6-0c2a5c67d926" :question/math-academy-id "q-360575" :question/problem "![](images/6f/6feb80156311c92f948b35742b39df3e6582c62a0d578481026b3141b01de3f1.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve \n$r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ),$\n represented by the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the first petal extends from the fourth quadrant to the first quadrant. Since the petal begins in the fourth quadrant, it is convenient to allow negative values of $\\theta $ when determining the interval.\n\nThe solutions for $6\\theta $ closest to $0$ on either side are\n\n$$\n6\\theta =-\\pi ,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for \n\n$$\n\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}].\n$$\n\nNotice that this interval matches the angular region traced by the shaded petal in the diagram.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5d5ccdb-070b-5ce1-b8af-dddb87675bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Cosine Polar Rose"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30b68c56-30ad-5a97-b72c-21abfa85a7c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /14}^{\\pi /14}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544195} {:answer/id #uuid "3d6c810c-df15-56ca-b73a-c69a46243cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /14}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544226} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5423ac-fe72-5892-8908-80ac3a1cfa10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /14}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544425} {:answer/id #uuid "bf25fda7-ec73-59b4-b184-e2d87135abad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_