eta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $7\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n7\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{14},\\frac{\\pi }{14}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{14},\\frac{\\pi }{14}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /14}^{\\pi /14}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}]}
